 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "17751a3b-ed4b-5966-a450-330460c02715"] :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=-10.$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-10\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-10x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-10x+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-10\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+Cx+D \\\\  & =-5{x}^{2}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-50139/ma-source-field/d50cb7942c0f354a9eeb3afec62053aeaef745ae8437be93c1dd3c76034f4883"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
