 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "38244b69-b2ff-532d-ba91-ffcf585c5c43"] :question/problem "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨{e}^{t}-4,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$, then $F(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨{e}^{t}-4,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨{e}^{t}-4,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int ({e}^{t}-4)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}-4t+{c}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}-4t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}-4t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-50202/ma-source-field/d54072a16f099b2b76723a2083a4b2545b899864f703a6aa1b916ab904356b56"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
