 {:db/id "q-64254/ma-source-field/4ab251dde99b3fdf526a92acc9ff43f6587a62452968dcfa9e156f6dacbfac48" :answer-field/id #uuid "1f8a8ed4-95f9-5ce5-8821-f45a9b7f474d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-64254/ma-source-field/4ab251dde99b3fdf526a92acc9ff43f6587a62452968dcfa9e156f6dacbfac48/dbbf552523e2b77610b46f6166a507d489a7f8b41418bb6a5b457c01b27a7387" "q-64254/ma-source-field/4ab251dde99b3fdf526a92acc9ff43f6587a62452968dcfa9e156f6dacbfac48/1404f3d3729d38c0a42f770c059db3dd2fc90c02f320711801f7e6cc341847b7" "q-64254/ma-source-field/4ab251dde99b3fdf526a92acc9ff43f6587a62452968dcfa9e156f6dacbfac48/239a7b64b769c1c3759cd853548d8c1fefc47d45b5465d1c23d73ba1c47e14a6" "q-64254/ma-source-field/4ab251dde99b3fdf526a92acc9ff43f6587a62452968dcfa9e156f6dacbfac48/17a585f7e449097654e6203a1cecfc345eb0add5b0dd21896ece23319aa2c53b" "q-64254/ma-source-field/4ab251dde99b3fdf526a92acc9ff43f6587a62452968dcfa9e156f6dacbfac48/ae4acecefd291bbb4c1267f64c71fe1373723e1ae6a91483805d82fa52cb9d25"] :answer-field/correct "q-64254/ma-source-field/4ab251dde99b3fdf526a92acc9ff43f6587a62452968dcfa9e156f6dacbfac48/239a7b64b769c1c3759cd853548d8c1fefc47d45b5465d1c23d73ba1c47e14a6" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "012482b3-0bd8-57c6-8936-9b9b3da520e7"] :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+144y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $8\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=144\\,⟹\\,\\omega =12.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡12t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $8\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=8.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=12R\\operatorname{cos}⁡12t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=8$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n8=12R\\,⟹\\,R=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡12t.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-64254/ma-source-field/4ab251dde99b3fdf526a92acc9ff43f6587a62452968dcfa9e156f6dacbfac48"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
