 {:db/id "q-65550" :question/id #uuid "2a31794b-90ed-5858-8a23-bda34267af75" :question/math-academy-id "q-65550" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c904a1eb628e726647a6f655a567a0939f7b7d404f0b32c6c4b4adaeca847916.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B-D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{4\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{4\\pi }{5})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $3$ units down. Hence, $D=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $B-D=(\\frac{5}{4})-(-3)=\\frac{5}{4}+3=\\frac{17}{4}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{5x}{4})-3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-65550/ma-source-field/a5b07ec82de3372a37f99ccb4c5981202564ebef0541a1de91da3bf0b3313e12"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
