 {:db/id "q-71396" :question/id #uuid "b0863b6f-83a0-5fff-bd6b-d5b930ec7673" :question/math-academy-id "q-71396" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(-1,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-1,0,1) & =2(-1)\\,i+2(0)\\,j+2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,i+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-2\\,i+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-71396/ma-source-field/90ea1f5b9b05685619d8c3316f3da00cea89de6a5764ba620b6df70f4730256f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
