 {:db/id "q-71399/ma-source-field/d193c0ffc41648c13993eb8fb5f6d5700ed892168930f8cea17c5cbad60bd5af" :answer-field/id #uuid "91095769-6695-5735-bdf7-d8d4bfdca38b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-71399/ma-source-field/d193c0ffc41648c13993eb8fb5f6d5700ed892168930f8cea17c5cbad60bd5af/39985d70ab4dfc808aa1f5d35d6745e4a01934de0e02ce0c162419cb6ed06f7b" "q-71399/ma-source-field/d193c0ffc41648c13993eb8fb5f6d5700ed892168930f8cea17c5cbad60bd5af/65804235d7bb4b17b7f416e60a2e70b14da5e8ccdcf4ea5163765c822cba76d5" "q-71399/ma-source-field/d193c0ffc41648c13993eb8fb5f6d5700ed892168930f8cea17c5cbad60bd5af/6c0cf2e3db20a0bea079d3658ec5b4df2ccf15a94420b1c5738af55d521bf260" "q-71399/ma-source-field/d193c0ffc41648c13993eb8fb5f6d5700ed892168930f8cea17c5cbad60bd5af/fc3cf9a9c9d0f107d15f4c431948eb277ad3e5d7c87f47ffc2e9cd7858d13f7d" "q-71399/ma-source-field/d193c0ffc41648c13993eb8fb5f6d5700ed892168930f8cea17c5cbad60bd5af/c1dce1f2a3d4009077e0c813dd56fd73b6c9f74650457c5a083f9a0b429122da"] :answer-field/correct "q-71399/ma-source-field/d193c0ffc41648c13993eb8fb5f6d5700ed892168930f8cea17c5cbad60bd5af/39985d70ab4dfc808aa1f5d35d6745e4a01934de0e02ce0c162419cb6ed06f7b" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-71399" :question/id #uuid "58a67c4a-aa8b-5b64-8268-56928e2716de" :question/math-academy-id "q-71399" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(0,0,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,k \\\\  & =({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }j+({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,0) & =({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,i+({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,j+({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-71399/ma-source-field/d193c0ffc41648c13993eb8fb5f6d5700ed892168930f8cea17c5cbad60bd5af"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
