 {:db/id "q-73894" :question/id #uuid "6fa85593-213a-592b-beaa-a7478f15ea2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73894" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e0466f463644e25cd2a615c445b6b32a6d9ae236c44658609cd778c5222fcae.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y={x}^{2}+3x$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-4$ to $x=-1$ is given by the integral expression below. What is the value of $a?$\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-4}^{a}({x}^{2}+3x)\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{a}^{-1}({x}^{2}+3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y={x}^{2}+3x.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2}+3x \\\\ 0 & ={x}^{2}+3x \\\\ x(x+3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $x=0$ or $x=-3.$ The only solution between $x=-4$ and $x=-1$ is $x=-3.$\n\nSince $-3\\in [-4,-1]$, we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-4$ to $x=-3.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=-1.$\n\nSo, $a=-3$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-4}^{-3}({x}^{2}+3x)\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}({x}^{2}+3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-73894/ma-source-field/d564a7afd754a23ea25838e9021e3b1becdeea043a7877efe0efabc2dcb3aa00"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
