 {:db/id "q-74316" :question/id #uuid "9af406e1-4ead-558e-9219-f0bb53a377fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-74316" :question/problem "Which of the following is a lower triangular matrix?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $B$ is a matrix for which all entries *above* the diagonal are $0$'s:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nHence, it is a lower triangular matrix. \n\nBy contrast, matrices $A$, $C$, $D$, and $E$ each have at least one non-zero entry above the diagonal:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,E=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -7 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, they are not lower triangular matrices." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-74316/ma-source-field/f71a14cfd5e38f5667ad5e1cfcd38a954d3d2638999ac9ad4fc67a92840c4a4b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
