 {:db/id "q-74781" :question/id #uuid "7e4e42a9-e81d-5610-8794-d5c4cdbe24ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-74781" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}21 \\\\ 17 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}]\\,$, find $\\,T(37{v}_{1}+39{v}_{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}37{v}_{1}+39{v}_{2} & =37{v}_{1}+37{v}_{2}+2{v}_{2} \\\\  & =37({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})+2{v}_{2} \\\\  & =37(0)+2{v}_{2} \\\\  & =2{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nT(37{v}_{1}+39{v}_{2})=T(2{v}_{2}).\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that $T(kv)=kT(v).$ So \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2{v}_{2}) & =2T({v}_{2}) \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}21 \\\\ 17 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}42 \\\\ 34 \\\\ -28\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, $T(37{v}_{1}+39{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}42 \\\\ 34 \\\\ -28\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-74781/ma-source-field/7901ac252fd88d2ca678066be87fd2b8b4f1d108105953de1807f67650431778"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
