 {:db/id "q-74782" :question/id #uuid "4bde10d1-5d59-51e1-a436-733e237150d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-74782" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\,$, find $\\,T(11{v}_{1}+6{v}_{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11{v}_{1}+6{v}_{2} & =11{v}_{1}+11{v}_{2}-5{v}_{2} \\\\  & =11({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})-5{v}_{2} \\\\  & =11(0)-5{v}_{2} \\\\  & =-5{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nT(11{v}_{1}+6{v}_{2})=T(-5{v}_{2}).\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that $T(kv)=kT(v).$ So \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-5{v}_{2}) & =-5T({v}_{2}) \\\\  & =-5[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-35 \\\\ -40 \\\\ -45\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, $T(11{v}_{1}+6{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-35 \\\\ -40 \\\\ -45\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-74782/ma-source-field/67a4938bb9be1fa7c427d98f7c3a8e3720c54329629c4695b69a758f496409d1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
