 {:db/id "q-74799/ma-source-field/dc9022d84a6050c3dfa686855614f8c96f848a1b4e8f65d3554addb73dc651a8" :answer-field/id #uuid "cbf795c1-51c1-5c95-aceb-7374460b2805" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-74799/ma-source-field/dc9022d84a6050c3dfa686855614f8c96f848a1b4e8f65d3554addb73dc651a8/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-74799/ma-source-field/dc9022d84a6050c3dfa686855614f8c96f848a1b4e8f65d3554addb73dc651a8/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-74799/ma-source-field/dc9022d84a6050c3dfa686855614f8c96f848a1b4e8f65d3554addb73dc651a8/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-74799/ma-source-field/dc9022d84a6050c3dfa686855614f8c96f848a1b4e8f65d3554addb73dc651a8/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-74799/ma-source-field/dc9022d84a6050c3dfa686855614f8c96f848a1b4e8f65d3554addb73dc651a8/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :answer-field/correct "q-74799/ma-source-field/dc9022d84a6050c3dfa686855614f8c96f848a1b4e8f65d3554addb73dc651a8/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "6b5fd0b7-3607-5c20-a6b8-4820e0b30df7"] :question/problem "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 10\\end{aligned}],$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=2,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-74799/ma-source-field/dc9022d84a6050c3dfa686855614f8c96f848a1b4e8f65d3554addb73dc651a8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
