 {:db/id "q-74983" :question/id #uuid "f4d629dc-c284-582e-8741-729f4784319a" :question/math-academy-id "q-74983" :question/problem "Given that  $\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}-5{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{3}\\in R}$, find a basis for $\\text{Null}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Any vector in $\\text{Null}(A)$ has the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nExpanding, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, a basis for $\\text{Null}(A)$ is\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nsince it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\text{Null}(A).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-74983/ma-source-field/3ee7bd790d01b63620f936782985389520bca99fc570078c8be95c8ea6e4a95a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
