 {:db/id "q-75002" :question/id #uuid "f9eeaf40-562a-5146-994e-535d231bf6bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-75002" :question/problem "Find a basis of the null space of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "To find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $Ax=0$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$\n\nThe given matrix is already in row echelon form. So, the equation $Ax=0$ corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=-9{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}=-3{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are basic variables corresponding to the pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd) and ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable corresponding to the non-pivot column. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9{x}_{3} \\\\ -3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{3}\\in R.$ This implies that\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate the $\\text{Null}(A).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-75002/ma-source-field/4e6e751fc9c0e1316b1dfd481881a2c88611ca72b1d769a4268e5dd7c743ecfb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
