 {:db/id "q-75177/ma-source-field/b53a11151424a90fe6b3a347ed58bf25ef5938f7a3103a4bb15f530d1b7b807a" :answer-field/id #uuid "63a6c2d7-95e4-53da-98cc-2de47ce76700" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-75177/ma-source-field/b53a11151424a90fe6b3a347ed58bf25ef5938f7a3103a4bb15f530d1b7b807a/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-75177/ma-source-field/b53a11151424a90fe6b3a347ed58bf25ef5938f7a3103a4bb15f530d1b7b807a/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" "q-75177/ma-source-field/b53a11151424a90fe6b3a347ed58bf25ef5938f7a3103a4bb15f530d1b7b807a/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-75177/ma-source-field/b53a11151424a90fe6b3a347ed58bf25ef5938f7a3103a4bb15f530d1b7b807a/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-75177/ma-source-field/b53a11151424a90fe6b3a347ed58bf25ef5938f7a3103a4bb15f530d1b7b807a/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5"] :answer-field/correct "q-75177/ma-source-field/b53a11151424a90fe6b3a347ed58bf25ef5938f7a3103a4bb15f530d1b7b807a/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-75177" :question/id #uuid "30a5731c-2a02-53f9-9c62-2c24fdfa7142" :question/math-academy-id "q-75177" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-\\frac{1}{2},$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-75177/ma-source-field/b53a11151424a90fe6b3a347ed58bf25ef5938f7a3103a4bb15f530d1b7b807a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
