 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "e1694262-cdf1-5325-9fc5-66702cda46da"] :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t}\\,,\\,\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}⟩,$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨0\\,,\\,1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{e}^{t}\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}\\,,\\,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $⟨0\\,,\\,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨0\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨0\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨0\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨{e}^{t}\\,,\\,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟩ & =0 \\\\ 0\\cdot {e}^{t}+1\\cdot \\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ t & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=0.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{e}^{0}\\,,\\,\\sqrt{1+{0}^{2}}⟩=⟨1\\,,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨0\\,,\\,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(1,1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-75519/ma-source-field/f2b38d9347a2ab25fa90f6fe8c865aeb849922a7eebdfa3eea066496651252e1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
