 {:db/id "q-76110/ma-source-field/253a5f36af83a0035576d9af980e33d26491daccdac76696453f54d027060d97" :answer-field/id #uuid "ad20513b-7f6b-51c1-b3e8-54f31e6cfac0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-76110/ma-source-field/253a5f36af83a0035576d9af980e33d26491daccdac76696453f54d027060d97/77a913a5c68cf3d8de28dce3d3292b3a50a20caba1be6fc314343612f38e3ea2" "q-76110/ma-source-field/253a5f36af83a0035576d9af980e33d26491daccdac76696453f54d027060d97/1fdb8dac9a33dff264a1441282384e6955f0294f914b3dca92b4510aa009c220" "q-76110/ma-source-field/253a5f36af83a0035576d9af980e33d26491daccdac76696453f54d027060d97/11948535861c8c617e49fd529fda48f2eb79ab2c4a2ee95d05f38328117ef743" "q-76110/ma-source-field/253a5f36af83a0035576d9af980e33d26491daccdac76696453f54d027060d97/cbf73d33747e7378dc321a3a36a200c81edd3097e93e1a1f09c97cdfaea27c83" "q-76110/ma-source-field/253a5f36af83a0035576d9af980e33d26491daccdac76696453f54d027060d97/92ceb4efdc0cd986fcf6d914c334e6bc24d60c60afe74f5d1910588d4d342e77"] :answer-field/correct "q-76110/ma-source-field/253a5f36af83a0035576d9af980e33d26491daccdac76696453f54d027060d97/77a913a5c68cf3d8de28dce3d3292b3a50a20caba1be6fc314343612f38e3ea2" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-76110" :question/id #uuid "bfd92570-bd08-5565-9c64-935de2ba2369" :question/math-academy-id "q-76110" :question/problem "![](images/76/760ddc657e8372a3860e2553e4f2b713a8b12544586c708e5f2c1922462567a8.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous? You may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "To determine the intervals of continuity of a function, we take the domain of the function and exclude any points of discontinuity.\n\nThe function is defined on the intervals $(-\\infty ,3),$  and $(3,\\infty ).$ There is a discontinuity at $x=3$  but this is already excluded.  \n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=-3,$ so we need to exclude that.\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-3,3),$  and $(3,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals. \n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $(-2,3)$ \n\nNotice that:\n\n- $(-3,4)$ and $(0,5)$ contain the excluded point $x=3,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals.\n\n- $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and  $(-\\infty ,0)$ contain the point of discontinuity $x=-3$, so the function is not continuous on these intervals." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-76110/ma-source-field/253a5f36af83a0035576d9af980e33d26491daccdac76696453f54d027060d97"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
