 {:db/id "q-78978/ma-source-field/95bc756a5ca2196b80a6e5a826a1516120747c6f34300e283aac0a2f51e0b5bb" :answer-field/id #uuid "c3d347a7-a48f-5cd4-9818-9dbb13f646cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-78978/ma-source-field/95bc756a5ca2196b80a6e5a826a1516120747c6f34300e283aac0a2f51e0b5bb/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-78978/ma-source-field/95bc756a5ca2196b80a6e5a826a1516120747c6f34300e283aac0a2f51e0b5bb/db7b536abbfbcf7bf94ee6f7b1bfcfcde80a4b0681c0d4a814e23a1769d0dc6f" "q-78978/ma-source-field/95bc756a5ca2196b80a6e5a826a1516120747c6f34300e283aac0a2f51e0b5bb/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-78978/ma-source-field/95bc756a5ca2196b80a6e5a826a1516120747c6f34300e283aac0a2f51e0b5bb/05402db46107d70ece210f0bd5e24b332ae4f43bd474d592035c3e3c92625629" "q-78978/ma-source-field/95bc756a5ca2196b80a6e5a826a1516120747c6f34300e283aac0a2f51e0b5bb/6ee96c99c6020a8a0675f054530cea7b5db72506dc41f7e364f7485717e0d433"] :answer-field/correct "q-78978/ma-source-field/95bc756a5ca2196b80a6e5a826a1516120747c6f34300e283aac0a2f51e0b5bb/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-78978" :question/id #uuid "f31a2fb0-549c-5b45-aecb-95fd4bb53cca" :question/math-academy-id "q-78978" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-12x+4$ have a local minimum?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-12 \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n3({x}^{2}-4)=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=\\pm 2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-3,0,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-3) & =3((-3)^{2}-4)=15>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3((0)^{2}-4)=-12<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =3((3)^{2}-4)=15>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-3$ |  | $0$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-2$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=2,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=2$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local minimum at $x=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-78978/ma-source-field/95bc756a5ca2196b80a6e5a826a1516120747c6f34300e283aac0a2f51e0b5bb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
