 {:db/id "q-78981/ma-source-field/8528a2c7f5cd2c762663e26bd9ca51eef943d40586e7c5b403dc797767f9cf5b" :answer-field/id #uuid "fa6e6554-f66a-52ad-babe-d98a19079136" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-78981/ma-source-field/8528a2c7f5cd2c762663e26bd9ca51eef943d40586e7c5b403dc797767f9cf5b/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-78981/ma-source-field/8528a2c7f5cd2c762663e26bd9ca51eef943d40586e7c5b403dc797767f9cf5b/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" "q-78981/ma-source-field/8528a2c7f5cd2c762663e26bd9ca51eef943d40586e7c5b403dc797767f9cf5b/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-78981/ma-source-field/8528a2c7f5cd2c762663e26bd9ca51eef943d40586e7c5b403dc797767f9cf5b/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-78981/ma-source-field/8528a2c7f5cd2c762663e26bd9ca51eef943d40586e7c5b403dc797767f9cf5b/74053cd8f212994373f56db80fbf0eade2779b20bfbeb0bc87c25a146cc918d6"] :answer-field/correct "q-78981/ma-source-field/8528a2c7f5cd2c762663e26bd9ca51eef943d40586e7c5b403dc797767f9cf5b/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-78981" :question/id #uuid "4bb06435-9175-533a-8727-5e983d5d29c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-78981" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=18x-2{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}$ have a local maximum?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =18-6{x}^{2}-12x \\\\  & =-6({x}^{2}+2x-3) \\\\  & =-6(x-1)(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-6(x-1)(x+3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3,1.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-3$ and $x=1.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-4,0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-4) & =-6(-4-1)(-4+3)=-30<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =-6(0-1)(0+3)=18>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-6(2-1)(2+3)=-30<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-4$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-3,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-3$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local maximum at $x=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-78981/ma-source-field/8528a2c7f5cd2c762663e26bd9ca51eef943d40586e7c5b403dc797767f9cf5b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
