 {:db/id "q-79494" :question/id #uuid "dcd4b923-bea2-5168-a3b2-3077cdbbb018" :question/math-academy-id "q-79494" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+81y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $3\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=81\\,⟹\\,\\omega =9.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $3\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=3.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=9R\\operatorname{cos}⁡9t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n3=9R\\,⟹\\,R=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-79494/ma-source-field/69015cf029c32ae7dce0b3e9818d84e67803ad5c4c988441e324b8b99383186f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
